                "body": {
                  "type": "text",
                  "settings": {
                    "text": "<p>The hotel opened in the 1870s as the Hôtel des Îles Britanniques, in the years when Sanremo filled each winter with the Russian and English courts. It became the Continental Palace, and took the name Miramare in 1935. White, Liberty in style, still hung with Murano glass.</p><p>Our pieces went into its public rooms and stayed a season. Then the hotel asked whether we would make the rest — the seating, the tables, the things a room is actually built from. Saying yes committed the house to a discipline it had not practised before.</p>",
